%[text] %[text:anchor:T_43E72092] # Coordinate System Transformations
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../../../Data/UniversalRobots/MainMenu_UR.m)
%[text] Building a robotic manipulator requires defining where each of its components are located in space, at all times. Each component will have an assigned coordinate system or frame. This script will introduce using coordinate systems and homogeneous transformation matrices for describing the position and orientation (or motion) of points (i.e., components) in cartesian space. At the end of this script, we will visualize the result of applying a homogenous transformation matrix to a defined point in space.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![point\_rotation.png](text:image:17d4)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] You will learn to construct transformation matrices to change between coordinate frames.
%[text] 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"} %[text:anchor:M_597CA885]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] **Before you get started:**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB Toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![](text:image:724f) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:4108) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2-hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8F8A032D] ![Warning symbol](text:image:345d)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and will generate errors if run out of order.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_14B46291] ## Introduction to Manipulators
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_1b8e] A generic robotic manipulator is a series of [links](internal:M_5759C0D4) attached by [joints](internal:M_8CA5476D) to a [base](internal:M_B4641675). They commonly also have an [end-effector](internal:M_7A2322C1), where a tool such as a drill or camera is mounted. Links can be rigid or flexible. For simplicity of solving kinematic equations, it is assumed that links be treated as rigid bodies. This means the links can be considered to have infinite stiffness. Therefore, an applied load on the link will result in zero deflection of material:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![manipulator\_model.png](text:image:6611)
%[text] There are also many types of joints used in robotics. Revolute, cylindrical, prismatic and spherical joints are some of the most used. Each joint is represented by a varying number of [degrees of freedom](internal:M_AEEF6726) (DOF): 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_E02B2CE6] ![](text:image:09e3)
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:5163) **Pro-tip**. For visualization purposes, a revolute joint can be thought of as a mechanism that functions like a door hinge. More info on the other types of joints also used in MATLAB can be found [here](https://www.mathworks.com/help/sm/referencelist.html?type=block&category=multibody-dynamics&s_tid=CRUX_topnav).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7CED996E] ## Coordinate Systems and Transformation Matrices
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_570F3233] Let's consider the mechanics of a ***simplified*** human arm (from the shoulder to the hand). We could model that arm as a robotic manipulator that has three revolute joints (following the diagram below). Traditionally, robotic arms are attached to a base platform that provides the rigid attachment for the robot to move. The first joint is connected to the base, rotating about angle $\\theta\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}. The second joint is a "shoulder" joint connecting the base and the first link, rotating about angle $\\theta\_2${"editStyle":"visual"}. The third joint is an "elbow" joint connecting the first link and the second link, rotating about angle $\\theta\_3${"editStyle":"visual"}. If we were to add a fourth joint to the end of the second link, it would be considered a "wrist" joint, connecting the second link to an end-effector, or hand. For the case of a robot manipulator that has three revolute joints, it is understood that it will have three rotational [degrees of freedom](internal:M_AEEF6726) (3DOF). If we added the fourth joint as mentioned above, the robot would have 4DOF. 
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![joint\_DOF.png](text:image:5734)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_A43882B7] Each joint is associated with its own cartesian coordinate system. The first joint connected to the base rotates about $\\theta\_1 \\;${"editStyle":"visual"}from the [base coordinate system](internal:M_AD05FF9C)$\\;\\left(x\_0 \\;,y\_0 \\;,z\_0 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} to the second joint coordinate system$\\;\\left(x\_1 \\;,y\_1 \\;,z\_1 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}. Similarly for the next two joints rotating about $\\theta\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} and $\\theta\_3${"editStyle":"visual"}, the coordinate systems need to be transformed in order to represent the location of the robot manipulator properly. Each joint coordinate system is specified by a pair of matrices $t${"editStyle":"visual"} and $R${"editStyle":"visual"}, where $t${"editStyle":"visual"} is a 3x1 translation vector and $R${"editStyle":"visual"} is a 3x3 rotation matrix.
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:572a) **Pro-tip**.  More advanced robots allow for 6DOF or more. Often, they have a spherical "wrist" joint at the end-effector which provides an additional three more degrees of freedom. The addition of spherical joints may more closely represent a real human arm's joints, adding to the complexity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B7526BDB] ### Coordinate Transformation: Translation
%[text] First, let us begin by understanding the translation vector. This is the amount the origin of frame $\\left(x\_1 \\;,y\_1 \\;,z\_1 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} or {1} has translated with respect to frame $\\left(x\_0 \\;,y\_0 \\;,z\_0 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} or {0}. In this case, the 3x1 translation vector or origin shift, $t\_1^{0\\;}${"editStyle":"visual"} is represented by $o${"editStyle":"visual"}:
%[text]  
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![point\_translation.png](text:image:4221)
%[text] 
%[text] Point $p\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}is defined by its position vector, $u${"editStyle":"visual"}. We can determine the position vector, $v${"editStyle":"visual"}, of the point $p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"} by adding the origin shift to the position vector in frame {1}. The position of point $p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"} is represented by a 3x1 vector. Using vector mathematics, we can represent the translation with: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_C3554ABC] $\\vec{v} =\\vec{o} \\;+\\vec{u}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] If we replace these vectors with our points of interest, we have:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_FB0AA108] $\\vec{p\_0 } =\\vec{o\_1^0 } \\;+\\vec{p\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:6f5c) **Pro-tip**. Point $p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"} is not a new point. Rather, it is a way of expressing the location of point $p\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} with respect to another frame of reference. In other words, point $p\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} with respect to frame {0} is equivalent to $p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"}.
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:1d38) **Demonstration**. Consider a given translated point $p\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} and an origin shift of a coordinate system $o\_1^0${"editStyle":"visual"}. What would be the position of point $p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"}?
% Point o
o =  [3;4;5]; %[control:editfield:6b41]{"position":[5,13]}

% Point p1
p1 = [1;2;1]; %[control:editfield:727c]{"position":[6,13]}

% Solving for point at (x0,y0,z0)
p0 = o + p1;
%[text] 
% Display figure
tFlag = true; %[control:checkbox:0bc3]{"position":[9,13]}

if tFlag
    % Display Point p0
    disp(string(['Point p1 with respect to frame {0}: ' 'p0 = ' '[' num2str(p0') ']']));

    hold on
    % Plot non-translated point p0
    fig1 = plot3(p0(1),p0(2),p0(3),'Marker','*',SeriesIndex = 7);
    text(p0(1),p0(2),p0(3),'     p0');
    
    % Plot translated point p1
    fig2 = plot3(p1(1),p1(2),p1(3),'Marker','*',SeriesIndex = 1);
    text(p1(1),p1(2),p1(3),'     p1');
    
    % Axes setting for coordinate frames
    ax1 = gca;
    quiverSize = max([range(ax1.XLim) range(ax1.YLim) range(ax1.ZLim)])/5;
    
    % Add coordinate frames to non-translated point    
    t1 = quiver3(p0(1), p0(2), p0(3), 1, 0, 0, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 1, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    t2 = quiver3(p0(1), p0(2), p0(3), 0, 1, 0, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 2, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    t3 = quiver3(p0(1), p0(2), p0(3), 0, 0, 1, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 3, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    
    % Add coordinate frames to translated point
    t4 = quiver3(p1(1), p1(2), p1(3), 1, 0, 0, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 1, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    t5 = quiver3(p1(1), p1(2), p1(3), 0, 1, 0, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 2, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    t6 = quiver3(p1(1), p1(2), p1(3), 0, 0, 1, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 3, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    
    % Add axes and legend info to plot
    fig1.Parent.XLabel.String = "X (m)";
    fig1.Parent.YLabel.String = "Y (m)";
    fig1.Parent.ZLabel.String = "Z (m)";
    fig1.Parent.XLim = [min(min(p0,p1))-1 max(max(p0,p1))+1];
    fig1.Parent.YLim = [min(min(p0,p1))-1 max(max(p0,p1))+1];
    fig1.Parent.ZLim = [min(min(p0,p1))-1 max(max(p0,p1))+1];
    legend(ax1,{'','','X','Y','Z'})
    view([46.91 35.45])
    grid
    hold off
else
    delete(fig1)
    delete(fig2)
    delete(ax1)
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C1F9A9A9] ### Coordinate Transformation: Rotation
%[text] Next, we will look at the rotation matrix. Compared to the translation example, we can see that the origin remains the same and the orientation shifts this time. If we rotate a frame $\\left(x\_1 \\;,y\_1 \\;,z\_1 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} or {1} with respect to frame $\\left(x\_0 \\;,y\_0 \\;,z\_0 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} or {0}, we need to apply a 3x3 rotation matrix, $R\_0^1${"editStyle":"visual"}.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![point\_rotation.png](text:image:6168)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] If a point $p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"} is to be measured from frame {1} with respect to frame {0}, the rotation is expressed as $\\Rightarrow${"editStyle":"visual"}$\\vec{p\_1 } =R\_0^1 \\cdot \\vec{p\_0 }${"editStyle":"visual"} where:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:5c5b)
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_359F5BF9] In order to create the rotation matrix $R\_0^1${"editStyle":"visual"} between frames {0} and {1}, each coordinate (with units of degrees) must be converted using a trigonometric rotation matrix specific to each point as follows [\[1\]](internal:M_E29AAF03):
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_55910F3C] $R\_x \\left(\\alpha \\right)=\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{ccc}\n1 & 0 & 0\\\\\n0 & \\cos \\alpha  & -\\sin \\alpha \\\\\n0 & \\sin \\alpha  & \\cos \\alpha \n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack${"editStyle":"visual"} $R\_y \\left(\\beta \\right)=\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{ccc}\n\\cos \\beta  & 0 & \\sin \\beta \\\\\n0 & 1 & 0\\\\\n-\\sin \\beta  & 0 & \\cos \\beta \n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack${"editStyle":"visual"} $R\_z \\left(\\gamma \\right)=\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{ccc}\n\\cos \\gamma  & -\\sin \\gamma  & 0\\\\\n\\sin \\gamma  & \\cos \\gamma  & 0\\\\\n0 & 0 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Combining all three matrices through multiplication provides a general rotation matrix to rotate all 3 points at the same time:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_C0E8334A] $R=R\_x \\left(\\alpha \\right)\\cdot R\_y \\left(\\beta \\right)\\cdot R\_z \\left(\\gamma \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:9ef2) **Pro-tip**. The sequence of rotation about the axes matters! The axes rotation shifts the coordinate system in different ways depending on the order of multiplication. E.g., starting the rotation with the y-coordinate $R\_y \\left(\\beta \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} instead of the x-coordinate $R\_x \\left(\\alpha \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}. The conventional rotation multiplication order is z, y, and then x.
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:49de) **Try**. Explore how a change in a coordinate frame's rotation angles affects the location of $p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"}.
%%
% XYZ coordinates to be rotated
x_rotation = 55;% degrees %[control:slider:11d3]{"position":[14,16]}
y_rotation = -100;% degrees %[control:slider:8302]{"position":[14,18]}
z_rotation = 145;% degrees %[control:slider:3bbb]{"position":[14,17]}

% Rotate each coordinate
[Rx,Ry,Rz] = rotate(x_rotation,y_rotation,z_rotation);

% Assembling Rotation Matrix
R_xyz = Rx*Ry*Rz;

% Point to be rotated
p_0 = [4;1;3];

% Perform rotation transformation
p_1 = R_xyz*p_0;
%[text] 
rotFlag = true; %[control:checkbox:0072]{"position":[11,15]}

if rotFlag
    % Display information about point p0 to be rotated
    disp(string(['Point to be rotated from coordinate frame {0}: ' 'p0 = ' '[' num2str(p_0') ']']));
    
    % Display information about the rotated point p1
    disp(string(['Point rotated to coordinate frame {1}: ' 'p1 = ' '[' num2str(round(p_1,2)') ']']));
    
    hold on
    % Plot non-rotated point
    fig4 = plot3(p_0(1),p_0(2),p_0(3),'Marker','*',SeriesIndex = 7);
    text(p_0(1),p_0(2),p_0(3),'  p0');

    % Plot rotated point
    fig5 = plot3(p_1(1),p_1(2),p_1(3),'Marker','*',SeriesIndex = 1);
    text(p_1(1),p_1(2),p_1(3),'  p1');
    
    % Axes setting for coordinate frames
    ax2 = gca;
    quiverSize = max([range(ax2.XLim) range(ax2.YLim) range(ax2.ZLim)])/5; 
    
    % Add coordinate frames to non-rotated point   
    q1 = quiver3(p_0(1), p_0(2), p_0(3), 1, 0, 0, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 1, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    q2 = quiver3(p_0(1), p_0(2), p_0(3), 0, 1, 0, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 2, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    q3 = quiver3(p_0(1), p_0(2), p_0(3), 0, 0, 1, quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 3, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    
    % Add coordinate frames to rotated point
    R1 = R_xyz*[1;0;0];
    R2 = R_xyz*[0;1;0];
    R3 = R_xyz*[0;0;1];
    q4 = quiver3(p_1(1), p_1(2), p_1(3), R1(1), R1(2), R1(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 1, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    q5 = quiver3(p_1(1), p_1(2), p_1(3), R2(1), R2(2), R2(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 2, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    q6 = quiver3(p_1(1), p_1(2), p_1(3), R3(1), R3(2), R3(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 3, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    view([-37.5000 30])

    % Reflection Example
    rFlag = true; %[control:checkbox:0d29]{"position":[13,17]}
    if rFlag
        R_yxz = Ry*Rx*Rz; % Assemble alternate Rotation Matrix based on y-coord
        p_1y = R_yxz*p_0; % Perform rotation
        M = [p_0,p_1,p_1y]; % Assemble Matrix for min/max of axes limits
        fig6 = plot3(p_1y(1),p_1y(2),p_1y(3),'Marker','*',SeriesIndex = 5);
        text(p_1y(1),p_1y(2),p_1y(3),'  p1*'); 
        fig6.Parent.XLim = [min(min(M))-2 max(max(M))+2];
        fig6.Parent.YLim = [min(min(M))-2 max(max(M))+2];
        fig6.Parent.ZLim = [min(min(M))-2 max(max(M))+2];
        R4 = R_yxz*[1;0;0];
        R5 = R_yxz*[0;1;0];
        R6 = R_yxz*[0;0;1];
        q7 = quiver3(p_1y(1), p_1y(2), p_1y(3), R4(1), R4(2), R4(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 1, 'LineWidth',1.5);
        q8 = quiver3(p_1y(1), p_1y(2), p_1y(3), R5(1), R5(2), R5(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 2, 'LineWidth',1.5);
        q9 = quiver3(p_1y(1), p_1y(2), p_1y(3), R6(1), R6(2), R6(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 3, 'LineWidth',1.5);
        legend(ax2,{'','','X','Y','Z'})
        grid
    else
        % Add axes and legend info to plot
        fig4.Parent.XLabel.String = "X (m)";
        fig4.Parent.YLabel.String = "Y (m)";
        fig4.Parent.ZLabel.String = "Z (m)";
        fig4.Parent.XLim = [min(min(p_0,p_1))-2 max(max(p_0,p_1))+2];
        fig4.Parent.YLim = [min(min(p_0,p_1))-2 max(max(p_0,p_1))+2];
        fig4.Parent.ZLim = [min(min(p_0,p_1))-2 max(max(p_0,p_1))+2];
        legend(ax2,{'','','X','Y','Z'})
    end
    grid
    hold off
else
    delete(fig4)
    delete(fig5)
    delete(ax2)
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:6da5) **Reflect**. We have seen how to rotate points from one frame to another using a combined rotation matrix. What happens when you rewrite the $R\_{\\mathrm{xyz}}${"editStyle":"visual"} equation as $R\_{\\mathrm{yxz}} =R\_y \\cdot R\_x \\cdot R\_z${"editStyle":"visual"}? Recalculate $p\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} and compare to the result of the original combined rotation matrix, $R\_{\\mathrm{xyz}}${"editStyle":"visual"}. What do you observe? For convenience, the code has already been written for you. Select the Reflection checkbox above to see the results.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BE68C5A7] ### Coordinate Transformation: Transformation Matrix
%[text] So far, we have seen translations and rotations of an individual coordinate frame, separately. In a typical robotic manipulator problem, you will have to translate and rotate to multiple frames. To simplify these operations for multiple frames, it is convenient to create a 4x4 transformation matrix $T${"editStyle":"visual"}, which includes both rotation *R* and translation *t* as follows:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_940ED4A4] $T${"editStyle":"visual"} = ${\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{cc}\nR^{3\\times 3}  & t^{3\\times 1} \\\\\n0^{1\\times 3}  & 1\n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack }^{4\\times 4}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] To calculate a forward transformation from coordinate frame {0} to coordinate frame {1}, we apply the following equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_45F95B2A] $p\_1 =T\_0^1 \\cdot p\_0${"editStyle":"visual"}, where $T\_0^1 =\\left\\lbrack \\begin{array}{cc}\nR\_0^1  & t\_0^1 \\\\\n0 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right\\rbrack${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] ![](text:image:5ed9) **Demonstration**. We will reuse the values from the previous translation and rotation exercises to calculate a forward transformation. Remember, we are rotating to frame {1} with respect to frame {0}.
%[text] 
R = R_xyz;    % 3x3 Rotation Matrix
t = o;       % 3x1 Translation Matrix
z = [0,0,0];  % 1x3 Zero Vector

% Assembling transformation matrix
T01 = [R t;z 1];

% 3x1 Point p position vector
pts = p0;
pM = padarray(pts,1,0,'post'); % 4x1 vector

% Forward Transformation
pM_ft = T01*pM;

% Non-transformed coordinate frame
nT1 = [1;0;0];
nT2 = [0;1;0];
nT3 = [0;0;1];

% Transformed coordinate frame
T1p = T01*[1;0;0;0];
T2p = T01*[0;1;0;0];
T3p = T01*[0;0;1;0];

% Unpad zero at the end of each transform vector
T1 = T1p(1:3); 
T2 = T2p(1:3);
T3 = T3p(1:3);

% Plot visualization of forward transformation matrix
aFlagFT = true; %[control:checkbox:58aa]{"position":[11,15]}

if aFlagFT
    
    hold on
    % Plot point p0
    f1 = plot3(pM(1),pM(2),pM(3),'Marker','*',SeriesIndex = 5);
    text(pM(1),pM(2),pM(3),'  p*');
    
    % Axes setting for coordinate frames
    ax3 = gca;
    quiverSize = max([range(ax3.XLim) range(ax3.YLim) range(ax3.ZLim)]*2); 
    
    % Add coordinate frames to non-transformed point   
    qft1 = quiver3(pM(1), pM(2), pM(3), nT1(1), nT1(2), nT1(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 1, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    qft2 = quiver3(pM(1), pM(2), pM(3), nT2(1), nT2(2), nT2(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 2, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    qft3 = quiver3(pM(1), pM(2), pM(3), nT3(1), nT3(2), nT3(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 3, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    
    % Package figure objects
    FigFT = [f1;qft1;qft2;qft3];
    
    % Assign names to axes and create legend
    f1.Parent.XLabel.String = "X (m)";
    f1.Parent.YLabel.String = "Y (m)";
    f1.Parent.ZLabel.String = "Z (m)";
    % Adjust limits to fit all point transforms
    f1.Parent.XLim = [min(min([pM pM_ft]))-5 max(max([pM pM_ft]))+5];
    f1.Parent.YLim = [min(min([pM pM_ft]))-5 max(max([pM pM_ft]))+5];
    f1.Parent.ZLim = [min(min([pM pM_ft]))-5 max(max([pM pM_ft]))+5];
    legend(ax3,{'','X','Y','Z'})
    view([-37.5000 30])
    grid
    
    % Animate transformation of points 
    animatePlot(FigFT,pM,pM_ft,nT1,nT2,nT3,T1,T2,T3,0);
    hold off
else
    delete(FigFT)
    delete(f1)
    delete(ax3)
end
%[text] 
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:543c) **Pro-tip**. T is a 4x4 matrix. Point p was originally a 3x1 vector (x,y,z coordinates of a point). In linear algebra, the multiplication of this matrix and vector cannot be done unless the matrix/vector inner dimensions are the same. Therefore, we must pad the end of the Point p vector with zeros to make it a 4x1 vector.
%[text] ![](text:image:5cb2) **Reflect**. Staying on the topic of manipulators, imagine what you are observing in the animation above is an end-effector moving from one position to another to complete a task.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0FF00CE2] ### Coordinate Transformation: Inverse Transformation Matrix
%[text] Imagine sitting down with your hand down by your side. Next, you raise your hand. Then, you put it back down. You just performed two transformations. Coming back to the theme of manipulators, we are now able to transform from coordinate frame {0} (hand down) to coordinate frame {1} (hand up). But how do we bring the manipulator back down? 
%[text] We can accomplish returning to coordinate frame {0} (hand down) by applying the inverse of the transformation matrix, $T^{-1}${"editStyle":"visual"}. You may have noticed the transformation matrix $T${"editStyle":"visual"} is a square matrix (4x4). This is special, because the way T is defined makes it possible to find an inverse of the matrix. The inverse can be expressed as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_CBF6EB9B] $p\_1 =T\_0^1 \\cdot p\_0 \\Longrightarrow p\_0 ={\\left(T\_0^1 \\right)}^{-1} \\cdot p\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}, where ${\\left(T\_0^1 \\right)}^{-1} =T\_1^0${"editStyle":"visual"} 
% Inverse matrix of T using built-in MATLAB functions
T10 = inv(T01); 

% Reverse Transformation
pM_inv = T10*pM_ft; %#ok<MINV> 

% Plot visualization of inverse transformation matrix
aFlagIT = true; %[control:checkbox:2cdc]{"position":[11,15]}

if aFlagIT
    
    hold on
    % Plot point p0
    f2 = plot3(pM_ft(1),pM_ft(2),pM_ft(3),'Marker','*',SeriesIndex = 5);
    text(pM_ft(1),pM_ft(2),pM_ft(3),'  p*');
    
    % Axes setting for coordinate frames
    ax4 = gca;
    quiverSize = max([range(ax4.XLim) range(ax4.YLim) range(ax4.ZLim)]*2); 
    
    % Add coordinate frames to non-transformed point   
    qit1 = quiver3(pM_ft(1), pM_ft(2), pM_ft(3), T1(1), T1(2), T1(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 1, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    qit2 = quiver3(pM_ft(1), pM_ft(2), pM_ft(3), T2(1), T2(2), T2(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 2, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    qit3 = quiver3(pM_ft(1), pM_ft(2), pM_ft(3), T3(1), T3(2), T3(3), quiverSize ,'SeriesIndex', 3, 'LineWidth',1.5);
    
    % Package figure objects
    FigINV = [f2;qit1;qit2;qit3];
    
    % Assign names to axes and create legend
    f2.Parent.XLabel.String = "X (m)";
    f2.Parent.YLabel.String = "Y (m)";
    f2.Parent.ZLabel.String = "Z (m)";
    % Adjust limits to fit all point transforms
    f2.Parent.XLim = [min(min([pM_ft pM_inv]))-5 max(max([pM_ft pM_inv]))+5];
    f2.Parent.YLim = [min(min([pM_ft pM_inv]))-5 max(max([pM_ft pM_inv]))+5];
    f2.Parent.ZLim = [min(min([pM_ft pM_inv]))-5 max(max([pM_ft pM_inv]))+5];
    legend(ax4,{'','X','Y','Z'})
    view([-37.5000 30])
    grid
    
    % Animate transformation of points
    animatePlot(FigINV,pM_ft,pM_inv,nT1,nT2,nT3,T1,T2,T3,1);
    hold off
else
    delete(FigINV)
    delete(f2)
    delete(ax4)
end
